A||||6||theogre||Note: Oil Filters||||1||
Z||000000||theogre||01-03-2001||08:59 PM||||If you've actually read the 4 cylinder recall info or the dipstick on recall service cars you may have seen that the oil filter is now suposed to be PF51.<P>Try finding one.....<P>All the filter makers, including ACDelco, list PF52 or equivalent.<P>So I asked a couple makers what the dif was if any. Acording to Purolator the only differance is that the PF52 and equivalent all have anti drain back valves. ACDelco hasn't responded yet.<P>Many of us already knew the 52's would work. It was just nice to confirm it better.||||reg||1
Z||000001||FieroJoe||01-03-2001||09:21 PM||||I actually found a PF51 at meijers when I went to get the filter for my first oil change that I did.  It looked really tiny, and the book on the shelf said PF52. My logic was more oil capacity = better.||||reg||1
Z||000002||LarryB||01-03-2001||10:50 PM||||The PF51 was discontinued several years ago, the PF52 is the correct recommended replacement.<P>The PF47 (the shorter filter) is still around.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||afRaceR||01-03-2001||11:24 PM||||Since we're kind of on the same subject, I have another question. This is to any of you guys who have engine oil coolers on your v6's. Where are you installing the actual cooler at? <P>I have been thinking about this for awhile and I don't want to run it all the way to the front but I can't really think of another location that would be suitable.||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||01-04-2001||07:56 PM||||Well I got ACDelco's responce. basically they say use the filter in the book. They also don't know anything about the recall...... (Niether did purolator.) On the other hand their legal department will lynch them if they depart from the book so.......<P>I can't see any reason we shouldn't use the long PF52 types. One of ACDelco's issues was clearance. Well thats a non issue on the 4 cylinder. Fact is the 47 is a _____ to get on and off. The 52 sticking out another couple inches makes a world of diferance.<P>Also the short filter is not even available in some lines. If you want a Castrol filter you get the long one for all the 4 cyl.||||reg||1
Z||000005||sluppy123||01-05-2001||05:31 PM||||afRaceR- I've been toying with the idea of a oil cooler for sometime. I plan to put the actual cooler in the passenger side scoop, but if you don't have side scoops I don't know where you could put it. <P>Have you looked into any of the cooler adapters? I've looked into most of the coolers and knowbody seems to make a adapter that will fit on the Fiero V-6(Clearance problems) I finally decided to make up my own adapter, I've got the design complete and just started on the prototype.<P>------------------<BR>-Brian, Toolmaker<BR>Lincoln, NE<BR>85' SC & 87' GT-Leather, Lowered and On The Bottle<BR> <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=247832&a=1843773&p=29628235&Sequence=0"> ||||reg||1
Z||000006||theogre||01-05-2001||06:37 PM||||Unless you've got a turbo setup or some other high heat issue like towing, oil coolers aren't needed. especially with synthetic oil. In fact if you don't keep the oil hot enough it can cause problems. Oil/air coolers can also slow warm up time.<P>I'd be more worried about getting heat out of the engine bay. Using the side scoop to alow more air into the bay will help cool pretty much everything. Anything that gets more cool air moving back there is a good thing.<P>That oil/water cooler someone has in another thread might be an option if you really want a cooler. I wouldn't add a cooler such that it heats air going into the engine bay. That area already runs over 200F real easy.||||reg||1
